   dev.pathland.view.dsl.Label("Songs") { dev.pathland.view.dsl.Icon(dev.pathland.view.core.IconName.MUSIC) }
 }.listStyle(dev.pathland.view.dsl.SidebarListStyle)
 val html = renderer().render(frameOf(root),1)
 assertTrue(html.contains("var(--pl-list-sidebar-selected-foreground"))
 assertTrue(html.contains("var(--pl-list-sidebar-icon-foreground"))
 assertTrue(html.contains("var(--pl-list-sidebar-selected-icon-foreground"))
 assertTrue(html.contains("var(--pl-space-6"))
 assertTrue(html.contains("var(--pl-space-3"))
 assertTrue(html.contains("var(--pl-control-font-size"))
}
```
Need know CSS var names: token path `control.font.size` -> `--pl-control-font-size`; `space.6` -> generative not var? For generative `space.6`, HTML token ref resolution uses `calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 6)` not `var(--pl-space-6)`. Our assertion should check `"calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 6)"`? Let's inspect HTML renderer token resolution for space. It likely emits `calc(var(--pl-space-base) * N)`. Design base CSS has `--pl-space-base`. The token ref for `space.6` resolves to `calc(...)`. So assert `"calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 6)"` and `* 3`. But HTML inline style may be `font-size:calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 6)`. Check renderer.

